DOGE-2 — Elon Musk, Special-Government-Employee Status, Operational Authority, and Private Financial Conflicts

I. Purpose of Exhibit

This exhibit examines Elon Musk’s formal governmental status, his publicly documented relationship with the Department of Government Efficiency, evidence concerning his actual operational authority, and potential conflicts arising from his continuing ownership or control of private companies substantially affected by federal contracts, regulations, investigations, and spending decisions.

The principal investigative questions are:

What federal position did Musk legally hold?
What authority was formally delegated to him?
Did his actual conduct exceed an advisory role?
Did federal agencies treat his statements or recommendations as directives?
Did he participate in matters affecting his private companies or competitors?
Did he receive ethics advice, recusals, waivers, or conflict determinations?
Did any government action produce a traceable private benefit?
Did Musk or associates participate in qualifying RICO predicate acts?

This exhibit does not conclude that Musk committed a criminal offense. It identifies evidence supporting further investigation and the substantial gaps that must be filled before alleging bribery, honest-services fraud, extortion, wire fraud, obstruction, or RICO conspiracy.

II. Formal Status as a Special Government Employee

The federal government formally classified Elon Musk as a special government employee (“SGE”) under 18 U.S.C. § 202(a).

An SGE is generally an officer or employee retained or appointed to perform temporary federal duties for no more than 130 days during a consecutive 365-day period. SGE status allows the government to obtain temporary assistance from individuals who may retain private employment and financial interests.

The classification does not exempt an SGE from all federal ethics requirements. Depending on the individual’s duties and the particular matter, potentially applicable provisions include:

18 U.S.C. § 208, concerning participation in matters affecting personal financial interests;

Federal bribery and gratuity prohibitions;
Restrictions on representing private parties before the government;
Misuse-of-position rules;
Nonpublic-information restrictions;
Financial-disclosure requirements;
Recusal obligations; and
Agency-specific ethics agreements or waivers.
The precise application of these rules depends on Musk’s appointment documents, expected service period, duties, financial-disclosure status, and participation in particular government matters.

Sources: 18 U.S.C. § 202, 18 U.S.C. § 208

III. Duration of Government Service

Musk’s formal government service began during the opening period of the Trump administration and ended in late May 2025.

On May 28, 2025, Musk publicly announced that his scheduled period as an SGE was ending. The White House subsequently held a public farewell event on May 30, 2025.

The expiration of Musk’s formal government status did not necessarily terminate:

His informal advisory relationship with President Trump;

His communications with remaining DOGE personnel;

The continued service of personnel associated with him;
Programs or directives initiated during his tenure;
Government use of systems or methodologies developed during that period; or
Potential legal responsibility for conduct occurring while he held government status.
Investigators must therefore distinguish among:
Musk’s formal SGE period;

Any governmental conduct before his formal appointment;

Informal advisory activity after his departure;
Continuing actions by personnel allegedly selected or supervised by him; and
Independent agency action merely consistent with his policy preferences.
A continuing political or advisory relationship is not sufficient to establish continued control of DOGE.
IV. Absence of Appointment as USDS Administrator

Executive Order 14158 formally created the position of USDS Administrator, reporting to the White House Chief of Staff.

The publicly available record did not identify Musk as the USDS Administrator. The administration instead described him as a senior adviser or special government employee.

This distinction is significant because the executive order assigned express authority to:

The President;

The White House Chief of Staff;

The USDS Administrator;

The Director of the Office of Management and Budget;
Agency heads; and
Agency DOGE team leads.
If Musk exercised authority assigned to one or more of these officers without holding the corresponding office or receiving a lawful delegation, the conduct could implicate constitutional or administrative-law questions.
However, an Appointments Clause or ultra vires violation is not automatically a RICO predicate. RICO requires separate proof that qualifying criminal acts were used to conduct the affairs of an enterprise.
V. Government’s Description of Musk’s Authority

The administration’s litigation position was that Musk served as a senior presidential adviser and did not possess independent authority to direct federal agencies.

A White House declaration submitted in litigation reportedly characterized Musk as:

A special government employee;

A senior adviser to the President;

Someone without authority to make government decisions independently;

Someone without authority to direct agencies or federal employees; and

Someone who was not the formal USDS Administrator.
This official description provides a potentially exculpatory explanation: Musk may have advocated policies and offered recommendations while agency heads or properly appointed officials made the ultimate decisions.
That defense must be tested against actual conduct. Formal job descriptions do not necessarily resolve who exercised operational control in practice.
VI. Public Evidence Suggesting Operational Influence
Public statements and litigation records produced substantial questions concerning whether Musk’s practical authority exceeded the government’s formal description.

In State of New Mexico v. Musk, the district court summarized allegations and public statements concerning Musk’s role. These included allegations that Musk:

Directed or coordinated the work of DOGE personnel;

Communicated directly with agency officials;

Pressured USAID officials concerning facility access;

Discussed the proposed shutdown of USAID with President Trump;

Publicly claimed that DOGE was terminating or stopping government payments;

Announced or took credit for contract cancellations;
Publicly declared “CFPB RIP” while DOGE-associated personnel were entering the agency;
Appeared jointly with the President to discuss DOGE’s activities; and
Was publicly described by White House personnel as having been tasked with starting DOGE.
The court was deciding a motion to dismiss. Its description of these matters did not constitute a final factual finding that every allegation was true.
Nevertheless, the court allowed significant portions of the constitutional claims against Musk and DOGE to proceed at that stage, while dismissing President Trump as a defendant.
Source: May 27, 2025 memorandum opinion in State of New Mexico v. Musk
VII. Actual-Control Analysis
Operational authority must be evaluated functionally rather than through titles alone.

Evidence of actual control could include proof that Musk:

Selected DOGE personnel;

Assigned DOGE personnel to particular agencies;

Established operational priorities;

Approved access to particular government systems;

Directed personnel or contract terminations;

Decided which payments should be stopped;

Ordered agency officials to provide records or facilities;
Threatened consequences for noncompliance;
Reviewed or approved DOGE public representations;
Received reports from agency DOGE teams;
Communicated instructions through intermediaries;
Controlled private communication channels used by DOGE personnel; or
Continued directing DOGE-associated personnel after ending government service.
Evidence weighing against actual control would include:
Written instructions limiting Musk to advisory functions;
Evidence that agency heads independently reviewed and approved decisions;
Records showing that the USDS Administrator controlled DOGE personnel;
Rejected recommendations from Musk;
Communications demonstrating that Musk lacked access to operational systems;

Evidence that DOGE personnel acted under agency-specific authority; and

Independent policy reasons for the challenged government actions.
VIII. The “Recommendation Versus Directive” Problem
One of the most important factual questions is whether Musk and DOGE made recommendations or issued commands.
In the DOGE FOIA litigation, the court recognized that the difference between a recommendation and directive could become unclear when agencies consistently followed DOGE’s proposals.
Relevant evidence includes:
Whether DOGE recommendations were routinely adopted;
Whether officials believed they could reject them;

Whether rejecting DOGE resulted in removal, reassignment, leave, or threatened consequences;

Whether agency counsel reviewed DOGE proposals;

Whether agency heads signed final decisions;

Whether those signatures reflected independent judgment or formal ratification of predetermined DOGE decisions;

Whether Musk communicated directly with the President before agency action; and

Whether agencies understood that Musk spoke with presidential authority.
A recommendation does not become a criminal command simply because it is influential. Investigators must identify the legal authority, coercive mechanism, decision-maker, and specific action.
IX. Relevant Private Financial Interests
During his government service, Musk retained substantial ownership or control interests in private companies whose operations were affected by federal contracting, regulation, licensing, investigations, procurement, and national policy.
The primary conflict-screening subjects include:
A. SpaceX and Starlink
Relevant federal interests potentially included:
Department of Defense contracts;

NASA contracts;

National-security launches;

Satellite communications;

Federal Communications Commission licensing;

Commerce Department broadband programs;

State Department support for overseas deployment;

Federal Aviation Administration oversight; and

Potential participation in new defense or missile programs.
B. Tesla
Relevant federal interests potentially included:
National Highway Traffic Safety Administration investigations;
Autonomous-vehicle regulation;
Federal Communications Commission matters;
Environmental regulation;
Labor enforcement;
Tax incentives;

Charging-infrastructure programs;

Tariff policy; and

Procurement of vehicles or related systems.
C. X Corp.
Relevant federal interests potentially included:
Federal-agency advertising;
Government use of X for public communications;
Federal Trade Commission obligations;
Government requests involving platform content;
Cybersecurity or privacy matters;
Foreign-investment and national-security questions; and

Potential influence produced by controlling a major communications platform.

D. xAI

Relevant federal interests potentially included:
Government artificial-intelligence contracts;
Access to federal datasets;
Data-center approvals and energy policy;
Defense and intelligence applications;
Federal AI procurement;
Government use of Grok or related services; and

Competitive effects from DOGE access to government technology systems.

E. Neuralink

Relevant federal interests potentially included:
Food and Drug Administration approvals;
Animal-welfare investigations;
Research oversight; and
Federal health or research policy.
F. The Boring Company
Relevant federal interests potentially included:

Federal transportation funding;

Infrastructure permits;

Environmental review;
State and local projects containing federal funds; and
Government contracting opportunities.
The existence of these interests does not prove self-dealing. It establishes the need for matter-specific conflict review.

X. Conflict-of-Interest Standard Under 18 U.S.C. § 208

Section 208 generally restricts an executive-branch employee from participating personally and substantially in a particular government matter when the matter would have a direct and predictable effect on the employee’s financial interests or certain imputed interests.

For investigative purposes, the following must be established:
Musk was a covered federal employee at the relevant time;
There was an identifiable particular matter;
Musk participated personally and substantially;
Musk possessed a covered financial interest;

The matter had a direct and predictable effect on that interest;

Musk acted knowingly; and

No applicable exemption, waiver, or recusal displaced the restriction.

Broad policy affecting an entire economic sector may be treated differently from a specific contract, enforcement matter, license, investigation, or proceeding involving a particular company.

Therefore, investigators must avoid treating every general regulatory change benefiting technology companies as a criminal conflict.

XI. Ethics Training, Disclosures, Waivers, and Recusals
GAO’s August 2026 report materially strengthens the need for additional investigation.
GAO identified 206 DOGE personnel, including 27 SGEs, but reported that Executive Office officials did not provide sufficient ethics-training and financial-disclosure records for GAO to determine whether all covered personnel complied.
The report did not find that Musk personally failed to complete a legally required filing. It demonstrated that GAO could not comprehensively verify the ethics and disclosure framework governing DOGE personnel.
For Musk specifically, investigators should obtain:
His appointment letter;
SGE designation;

Position description;

Beginning and ending service dates;

Anticipated-duty-day calculation;

Confidential or public financial-disclosure reports;

Ethics-training certification;

Ethics opinions;

Recusal statements;

Section 208 waivers;

Authorization to participate in specific matters;
Communications with designated agency ethics officials; and
His offboarding and continuing-adviser documentation.
Source: GAO-26-108403 — DOGE Personnel and Ethics Activities
XII. Federal Contracts and the Private-Benefit Question
Musk-controlled businesses had extensive relationships with the federal government before DOGE was established. Continued or additional contracts during his government service do not, standing alone, establish corruption.
The necessary analysis is transactional.
For every relevant award, investigators should determine:
Which agency issued the award;
When the procurement began;
Whether it was competitively bid;
Who evaluated the proposals;
Whether DOGE personnel influenced the procurement;

Whether DOGE recommended eliminating competitors or alternative programs;

Whether Musk accessed nonpublic procurement information;

Whether any official was pressured to favor a Musk-controlled entity;

Whether Musk participated in a policy directly affecting the award;

Whether a waiver or recusal applied; and

Whether the government received fair value.

The strongest evidence of improper benefit would not be the mere existence of a contract. It would be proof connecting a governmental act by Musk or DOGE to a particular financial advantage for Musk, his companies, or another enterprise participant.
XIII. Regulatory and Enforcement Benefits
Potential private benefit could also arise through regulatory or enforcement action.
Investigators should compare DOGE activities against pending or anticipated matters involving Musk-controlled entities, including:
Enforcement investigations;
Safety inquiries;
Labor proceedings;
Environmental reviews;
Licensing decisions;
Consent-decree compliance;
Grant eligibility;

Procurement standards;

Autonomous-vehicle rules;

Satellite and spectrum matters;

AI procurement requirements; and

Agency staffing reductions affecting regulators responsible for Musk’s companies.

For each matter, the evidence must show more than temporal overlap. Investigators must establish:
Musk’s knowledge of the matter;
Musk’s participation in the relevant government decision;
Communications identifying the private interest;
The decision’s direct effect;
Departure from ordinary procedures;
Concealment or false representations;
A corrupt benefit or agreement; and
Any applicable RICO predicate.
XIV. Access to Nonpublic Government Information
DOGE teams received or sought access to sensitive federal systems containing:
Payment information;

Government-contract information;

Personnel records;
Social Security information;
Business records;
Regulatory information;
Grant information;
Sensitive program data; and
Potentially proprietary information supplied by private contractors.
The investigative concern is whether any such information was:

Accessible to Musk;

Transmitted to a Musk-controlled company;

Used to train or improve private AI systems;

Used to identify commercial opportunities;
Used to disadvantage competitors;
Retained after the government purpose ended;
Exported to private devices or accounts; or
Used in securities, contracting, investment, or acquisition decisions.
No public evidence reviewed here establishes that Musk transferred federal data to his companies. This remains an evidentiary question requiring access logs, communications, device records, data-loss-prevention logs, and forensic comparison.
XV. Campaign Support and Quid-Pro-Quo Analysis
Musk provided substantial political and financial support for Trump’s 2024 campaign and allied political efforts. After the election, he received extraordinary access to the administration and a temporary governmental role.
Political support followed by appointment or influence does not itself prove bribery.

To establish a corrupt quid pro quo, investigators would need evidence that:

A contribution or other thing of value was given in exchange for an agreed governmental act;
The government act was sufficiently specific;
The relevant parties shared a corrupt understanding;
Musk intended the contribution or assistance as payment rather than political support; and
The official accepted it on that basis.
Circumstantial evidence may contribute to this analysis, but timing, access, and favorable treatment alone are not enough.
XVI. Potential RICO Predicate Theories
A. Honest-services fraud

A possible theory would require proof that Musk or another participant used mail or wire communications to facilitate a bribery or kickback scheme involving official action.

Undisclosed conflicts, favoritism, or violations of ethics rules are not sufficient by themselves after Skilling v. United States. A bribe or kickback must be established.

B. Wire or mail fraud

Investigators might examine whether material false representations about authority, savings, conflicts, data use, or government decisions were transmitted to obtain money or property.

The government’s or public’s general right to honest administration is not property for ordinary fraud purposes. The alleged scheme must be directed at traditionally recognized property.

C. Hobbs Act extortion

A potential theory could arise if official authority or threatened government action was used to obtain property for Musk, a related company, or another enterprise participant.
Aggressive policy pressure or threats to terminate employment do not automatically constitute Hobbs Act extortion. Investigators must prove the “obtaining of property” element.
D. Obstruction
Obstruction could become relevant if records were intentionally destroyed, concealed, falsified, or withheld with a corrupt nexus to a qualifying federal proceeding.
Using Signal or another messaging platform is not automatically obstruction. The required proof includes preservation duties, the existence or foreseeability of a proceeding, intentional destruction or concealment, and corrupt purpose.

E. Witness or informant retaliation

If Musk or associates retaliated against a person because that person supplied information concerning a possible federal offense, investigators must establish:

A protected disclosure;

Recipient knowledge;

Actor identity;

A prohibited retaliatory action;

Intent;

A causal connection; and

The applicable federal provision.

F. RICO conspiracy

A RICO conspiracy requires evidence that Musk agreed that members of the enterprise would conduct its affairs through a pattern of racketeering activity.

An agreement to restructure government, reduce spending, support the President, or advance shared policy objectives is not a criminal RICO agreement.

XVII. Evidence Supporting Further Investigation

The following public circumstances justify continued investigation:

Musk’s formal SGE designation;

His unique access to the President;

Public statements associating him with DOGE leadership;

DOGE personnel reportedly connected to Musk or his companies;
His public claims concerning government payments, agencies, and contracts;
Litigation allegations that he directed DOGE activities;
The discrepancy between his publicly perceived role and the government’s formal description;
His continuing ownership of companies regulated by and contracting with the federal government;
Incomplete public visibility into DOGE ethics records;
DOGE access to sensitive government systems;

Government actions affecting agencies regulating Musk’s businesses;

Continued influence after Musk’s formal departure; and

The persistence of DOGE-associated personnel within federal agencies.

These circumstances establish investigative relevance, not guilt.

XVIII. Evidence That Could Defeat or Narrow the Theory

A complete investigation must also preserve exculpatory evidence, including:

Proof Musk acted only as a presidential adviser;
Records showing agency heads made independent decisions;
Evidence Musk lacked system access;
Binding recusals covering matters affecting his companies;
Section 208 waivers;
Independent procurement processes;
Preexisting contracts initiated before his appointment;
Policy changes affecting entire industries rather than particular companies;
Rejected Musk recommendations;
Legal advice authorizing challenged conduct;
Evidence that private companies received no DOGE information;
Proof that public statements were political rhetoric rather than operational directives; and
Appellate decisions reversing or narrowing district-court rulings concerning DOGE authority.

XIX. Required Investigative Materials

Musk’s complete appointment and offboarding files;

His SGE day-count records;

Position description and delegation documents;

Financial-disclosure forms;
Ethics training and counseling records;
Recusal agreements and Section 208 waivers;
Communications with the President and White House Chief of Staff;
Communications with the USDS Administrator;
Communications with OMB and OPM;
Communications with agency DOGE teams;
Communications with SpaceX, Tesla, X, xAI, Neuralink, and The Boring Company;
DOGE personnel-selection records;
Contract and grant decision files affecting Musk companies or competitors;
Regulatory and enforcement records involving those companies;
Government-system access logs;
Data-export and removable-media logs;

Government and private-device forensic records, obtained lawfully;

Signal, email, text, and collaborative-document preservation records;

Calendars, visitor logs, and meeting records;
Communications surrounding USAID and CFPB actions;
DOGE public-post approval records;
Government advertising or communications purchases involving X;
AI-related contracts and datasets;
Documents concerning post-government advisory activities; and
Evidence of any financial benefit tied to a specific DOGE action.
XX. Preliminary Investigative Finding
The public record establishes that Musk was a special government employee with extraordinary presidential access, substantial public influence over DOGE, and continuing financial interests in companies deeply affected by federal activity.
The record also presents a material discrepancy between:
Public statements and conduct portraying Musk as DOGE’s operational leader; and
The government’s litigation position that he possessed only advisory authority and could not independently direct agencies.
That discrepancy warrants investigation into Musk’s actual functional authority.
The available evidence does not yet establish that Musk received a bribe, accepted a kickback, obtained government property, extorted private property, obstructed a federal proceeding, transferred government data to his companies, or agreed to conduct DOGE’s affairs through racketeering predicates.
The potential RICO theory becomes materially stronger only if subsequent evidence connects:
Musk’s governmental participation → a specific official action → a particular private benefit → concealment or corrupt agreement → a qualifying predicate act.

