DOGE-32 — Federal Reserve Master-Account Access, Nonbank Fintech Privileges, Stablecoin Settlement, and the Alleged Creation of Private Central-Bank-Money Gateways

I. Investigative purpose

This section examines whether the administration’s financial-technology policy is creating a pathway through which stablecoin issuers, cryptocurrency companies, uninsured digital-asset banks, or other nonbank entities could obtain direct or functionally equivalent access to Federal Reserve accounts and payment services.

The principal questions are whether private digital-asset firms could obtain:

Direct access to Federal Reserve master accounts;
Direct participation in Fedwire, FedACH, FedNow, or related services;
Settlement in central-bank money without an ordinary sponsoring bank;
Preferential access through affiliated or specially chartered institutions;
Stablecoin minting and redemption linked directly to Reserve Bank funds;
Access without the capital, liquidity, supervision, and public obligations imposed on insured banks; and
A privileged government-supported gateway connecting private tokens to sovereign payment infrastructure.

The public evidence establishes an executive-branch effort to review and potentially expand access. It does not establish that Coinbase, Circle, or another ordinary nonbank company presently possesses a Federal Reserve master account.

II. What a Federal Reserve master account is

A master account is an account maintained by an eligible financial institution at one of the twelve Federal Reserve Banks.

It functions as the institution’s settlement account within the Federal Reserve system and can support access to services involving:

Fedwire Funds Service;

FedACH;
Check settlement;
National Settlement Service;
FedCash;
Securities settlement;
Correspondent relationships;
FedNow; and
Other Reserve Bank payment services.
Balances in a master account constitute central-bank money—a direct position within the Federal Reserve payment system rather than a claim against a private correspondent bank.

A master account is therefore materially different from:

A commercial bank account;

A stablecoin reserve account;
A crypto-exchange wallet;
A Treasury account;
A custodial omnibus account; or
Technical connection to a payment processor.
III. Three access levels that must not be conflated

Access levelDescriptionLegal and operational significanceDirect master accountInstitution holds its own account at a Reserve BankDirect settlement relationship with the Federal ReserveCorrespondent accessInstitution uses another bank’s master accountSponsor bank controls or intermediates accessTechnical network accessFintech supplies software or connects through a participantDoes not necessarily hold central-bank money or a master account

A company may advertise “FedNow connectivity,” “real-time settlement,” or “Federal Reserve payment access” while operating entirely through a sponsoring bank.
Investigators must identify the legal account holder, not merely the customer-facing technology provider.
IV. Existing Federal Reserve access principles
In 2022, the Federal Reserve Board adopted final guidelines for evaluating requests for Reserve Bank master accounts and services.
The guidelines identify six central principles:
The applicant must be legally eligible under the Federal Reserve Act or another federal statute.
Access must not create undue credit, operational, settlement, cyber, or other risks to a Reserve Bank.
Access must not create undue risks to the payment system.
Access must not create undue risks to financial stability.
Access must not facilitate money laundering, terrorism financing, fraud, cybercrime, or other illicit conduct.
Access must not interfere with monetary-policy implementation.
The guidelines permit Reserve Banks to impose conditions, restrictions, or risk controls and to deny applications presenting risks that cannot be adequately mitigated. Federal Reserve Account Access Guidelines
V. The three-tier review framework
The Federal Reserve guidelines use a tiered review structure.
Tier 1
Federally insured institutions subject to a well-established federal prudential framework generally receive the most streamlined review.

Tier 2

Eligible but uninsured institutions subject to federal prudential supervision receive an intermediate level of review.

Tier 3

Eligible institutions that are neither federally insured nor subject to comparable federal prudential supervision receive the strictest review.

Digital-asset special-purpose banks and other novel institutions may fall into the higher-scrutiny categories depending on their charter, insurance status, supervision, holding-company structure, and business model.

The framework reflects a critical principle: an institution’s legal eligibility to apply does not automatically entitle it to approval under the Federal Reserve’s present interpretation.

VI. Executive Order 14405
On May 19, 2026, President Trump issued Executive Order 14405, Integrating Financial Technology Innovation Into Regulatory Frameworks.
The order directed federal financial regulators to review and reduce regulations and supervisory practices that impede fintech firms from partnering with regulated financial institutions.
It expressly encompassed:
Payment processing;
Digital banking;

Deposit-taking;

Investment management;

Brokerage;

Custody;

Digital-asset services;

Blockchain services; and

Other financial activities.

Most significantly, it requested that the Federal Reserve evaluate the legal and policy framework governing access to Reserve Bank payment accounts and services by:

Uninsured depository institutions;

Nonbank financial companies;

Digital-asset firms;

Other novel financial firms; and

Entities acting as direct participants in real-time payment networks.

Executive Order 14405

VII. What Executive Order 14405 did not do

The order did not itself:

Open a Federal Reserve account;

Approve any applicant;
Amend the Federal Reserve Act;
Expand statutory eligibility;
Order a Reserve Bank to approve Coinbase or Circle;
Guarantee access to a stablecoin issuer;
Waive the 2022 account-access guidelines;
Convert stablecoins into central-bank liabilities; or
Authorize nonbanks to receive federal deposit insurance.
It requested an evaluation and recommendations.

Therefore, any claim that the order “gave crypto companies Federal Reserve master accounts” would be inaccurate.

VIII. The Federal Reserve review deadline
Executive Order 14405 requested that the Federal Reserve submit its findings, options, and recommendations within 120 days of May 19, 2026.
That period runs to approximately September 16, 2026. As of September 6, 2026, the requested period has not yet expired.
The review is supposed to address:
The Federal Reserve’s existing legal authority;

Options for expanded access;

Legal impediments;

Possible legislative changes;

Risk-management requirements;

Financial-stability consequences;
Access to real-time payment systems; and
Whether individual Reserve Banks possess independent authority to approve or deny applicants.
The eventual report, drafts, communications, and supporting analyses will be central DOGE-32 evidence.
IX. The potential stablecoin gateway
If a stablecoin issuer or affiliated institution obtained direct Federal Reserve access, the settlement chain could become:
flowchart TD
    A["Federal Reserve master account"] --> B["Stablecoin issuer or affiliated institution"]
    B --> C["Customer dollar received"]
    C --> D["Corporate token minted"]
    D --> E["Private blockchain circulation"]
    E --> F["Token redeemed"]
    F --> B
This would not make the stablecoin itself central-bank money.
It would, however, allow a private token to be minted and redeemed through an institution settling directly in central-bank money, eliminating or reducing reliance on intermediary commercial banks.

That could materially strengthen:

Redemption reliability;

Payment speed;

Institutional acceptance;

Reserve liquidity;

Market confidence;

Competitive position; and

The public perception that the stablecoin is effectively government supported.
X. Direct central-bank access as a public franchise
A master account is not merely an ordinary vendor relationship.
It provides access to infrastructure created and maintained under federal law. That access can confer:
Settlement finality;
Reduced intermediary risk;
Lower payment costs;
Direct access to essential payment services;

Increased institutional legitimacy;

Improved liquidity management;

A competitive advantage over firms dependent on correspondent banks; and

Greater resilience against private-sector account termination.

Accordingly, master-account access can operate like a valuable public franchise.
The investigation must determine whether access decisions are made according to neutral, publicly defined risk criteria or through political relationships and industry influence.
XI. Stablecoin reserves and central-bank settlement
A stablecoin issuer without direct Federal Reserve access generally depends on commercial banks to:
Receive customer deposits;
Hold reserve cash;
Process wires;
Settle redemptions;
Access Fedwire and FedACH;

Supply intraday liquidity; and

Maintain correspondent relationships.

Direct access could permit a qualified issuer or affiliate to reduce these dependencies.

Potential benefits include:
Faster minting after receipt of funds;
Faster redemptions;
Reduced bank-failure exposure;
Better weekend and after-hours liquidity;
Lower fees;
Clearer settlement records; and

Less concentration among sponsoring banks.

Potential risks include:

Crypto-related runs entering central-bank systems directly;

Intraday overdraft exposure;

Rapid transmission of blockchain-market shocks;
Weaker supervision than applies to insured banks;
Stablecoin issuers becoming systemically important;
Perceived government endorsement;
Cyberattacks against direct participants; and
Pressure for emergency Federal Reserve support.
XII. The “private CBDC” problem
A privately issued stablecoin is not technically a CBDC because it is not a direct liability of the Federal Reserve.

But a stablecoin can acquire CBDC-like functional characteristics if:

It is denominated in dollars;

It is redeemable at par;

Its issuer settles directly through a Reserve Bank;

It is accepted throughout the payment system;

Government agencies accept or distribute it;
Its reserves are primarily Treasury and central-bank assets;
It operates continuously; and
Its issuer can freeze or reprogram balances.
The result could be a private digital-dollar network connected directly to central-bank settlement.
This is more precise than calling USDC or another private token a literal CBDC.
XIII. Public versus private accountability

Public CBDC modelPrivate stablecoin with Fed accessCentral bank issues liabilityCorporation issues redemption claimPublic governanceCorporate governance plus regulatory supervisionMonetary economics remain publicReserve economics remain largely privatePublic-law transparencyCorporate confidentiality and regulatory reportingGovernment controls technical standardsPrivate issuer controls token designConstitutional constraints apply directlyState-action status may be disputedPublic audit and legislative oversightPrivate audit, examination, and selective disclosureDirect sovereign responsibilityGovernment disclaims stablecoin guarantee

If private stablecoins receive central-bank access while a federal CBDC remains prohibited, the government may confer many practical benefits of sovereign digital money without assuming corresponding public accountability.

XIV. The Custodia Bank precedent
Custodia Bank, a Wyoming-chartered special-purpose depository institution oriented toward digital-asset companies, applied for a Federal Reserve master account.
Its request was ultimately denied. Litigation followed concerning whether an eligible state-chartered institution was entitled to an account or whether a Reserve Bank retained discretion to reject the request.
The prevailing panel position recognized Reserve Bank discretion. In March 2026, the Tenth Circuit denied rehearing en banc. A dissent argued that Custodia was legally eligible and that denial of a master account was effectively a veto over a state’s chartering authority. Custodia rehearing order and dissent
The precedent is important because it shows:
Digital-asset account access is not automatic;
Eligibility and approval are separate questions;

Reserve Banks presently possess significant decision-making authority;

State-chartered crypto institutions may receive the strictest scrutiny; and
Executive or legislative intervention could materially change the existing balance.
XV. Selective reversal concerns
Investigators should determine whether Executive Order 14405 or subsequent Federal Reserve actions produced a general neutral framework or selectively benefited politically connected firms.
Questions include:
Were previously denied applicants invited to reapply?
Were access standards changed after political contributions?
Were review timelines shortened for favored companies?

Were particular charter structures designed around preferred applicants?

Did regulators waive holding-company supervision?

Were uninsured firms given services unavailable to ordinary community banks?

Did White House officials communicate about specific applications?

Were career Federal Reserve objections overridden?
Did applicants receive nonpublic guidance?
Were competing stablecoin issuers treated differently?
A policy reversal is not itself corrupt. Selective treatment tied to political or financial benefits could support a stronger legal theory.
XVI. Coinbase and Circle
The Federal Reserve’s public access-request database did not show obvious entries under the names “Coinbase” or “Circle” in the material reviewed for this section.
That does not establish that the companies have no indirect or affiliated access. Relevant possibilities include:
Access through commercial-bank partners;

Access through a separately chartered subsidiary;

Access through BNY or another custodian;

Technical access through a payment processor;

Use of omnibus accounts;
A pending application under another legal name; or
Future acquisition of an eligible institution.
Investigators must identify every affiliate, charter, sponsoring bank, correspondent account, and Reserve Bank relationship rather than searching only the companies’ public brands.
XVII. BNY as an indirect central-bank gateway
BNY already provides reserve custody and institutional stablecoin services involving USDC. It also participates in traditional central-bank payment infrastructure as a major regulated banking institution.
Through such an arrangement:
Circle may remain the stablecoin issuer.
BNY may hold reserve assets or fiat.
Institutional customers may instruct mints and burns through BNY.
BNY may settle the fiat side through its banking and Federal Reserve relationships.
USDC circulates through private blockchain networks.
This can provide Circle with much of the practical benefit of central-bank access without Circle holding a master account itself.
The alleged private gateway may therefore exist through a bank–fintech partnership even if the fintech’s own master-account application never exists.
XVIII. Correspondent-bank dependency
Using a sponsoring or correspondent bank preserves a regulated intermediary between the fintech and the Federal Reserve.
The sponsor bank can:
Conduct due diligence;
Monitor transactions;
Impose limits;
Terminate service;

Supply liquidity;

Submit payment instructions;

Maintain the master account; and

Bear direct responsibility to the Reserve Bank.

Digital-asset firms argue that this structure creates:

Higher costs;

Delayed settlement;

Debanking risk;
Concentration among sponsor banks;
Limited weekend access; and
Dependence on a competitor or gatekeeper.
The regulatory question is whether those burdens are unnecessary barriers or essential safeguards.

XIX. FedNow participation

FedNow is an instant-payment service operated by the Federal Reserve Banks.

A fintech may provide a customer-facing interface for FedNow without being the legal participant holding the underlying Federal Reserve account.

Investigators must distinguish:

A direct FedNow participant;
A service provider supporting a participant;
A correspondent respondent;
A wallet interface;
A payment-message provider; and
A stablecoin settlement system advertising “instant” payment.
Marketing references to FedNow should be reconciled against the Reserve Bank account and service records.
XX. Monetary-policy implications
Broad nonbank access can affect monetary policy because master-account balances interact with the Federal Reserve’s balance sheet and payment operations.
Potential questions include:

Would stablecoin issuers earn interest on Reserve Bank balances?

Would the Federal Reserve impose non-interest-bearing account conditions?

Would balances migrate from commercial-bank deposits?

Would stablecoin growth reduce bank funding?

Could issuers demand emergency liquidity?

Would reserve requirements apply?
Could rapid token redemptions produce large Reserve Bank outflows?
Would direct access alter the Federal Reserve’s control of short-term rates?
Could stablecoin firms become counterparties in monetary operations?
These issues should be resolved publicly before any private issuer obtains access capable of supporting large-scale retail tokens.
XXI. Deposit flight and bank disintermediation
Direct stablecoin access could accelerate movement from bank deposits into corporate tokens.

A depositor might transfer money from a community bank into a stablecoin whose reserves are held through:

A master account;

Large custodial banks;

Treasury securities;

Government money-market funds; or

Centralized repo markets.
That movement could:
Reduce deposits available for local lending;
Concentrate funds in large institutions;
Increase Treasury demand;

Increase stablecoin reserve income;

Weaken community-bank funding;

Move credit creation toward capital markets; and

Increase government pressure to support stablecoin infrastructure during stress.

The issue is not merely payment innovation. It concerns redistribution of the banking system’s funding base.

XXII. Intraday credit and overdraft exposure

Reserve Banks sometimes face credit risk when account holders send payments exceeding available balances or receive provisional credit.
Before granting access to a stablecoin-related institution, regulators should determine:
Whether daylight overdrafts are permitted;
What collateral is required;
Whether the account must remain prefunded;
Whether token issuance can occur against pending payments;
Whether reserve balances are segregated;
Whether customer funds secure Federal Reserve exposure;

Whether transaction limits apply;

Whether weekend minting creates unmatched liabilities; and
Whether automated suspension occurs when balances are insufficient.
A private issuer should not be able to mint immediately spendable tokens using implicit Federal Reserve credit without express statutory authorization and public disclosure.
XXIII. Illicit-finance and sanctions risks
The Federal Reserve guidelines require consideration of whether access could facilitate:
Money laundering;

Terrorism financing;

Fraud;

Cybercrime;

Sanctions evasion; or

Other illicit activity.

Stablecoin settlement can improve traceability because blockchain transactions are recorded. But rapid, international, pseudonymous transfers can also accelerate movement before an issuer or law-enforcement agency acts.

Any applicant should disclose:
Customer-identification standards;
Wallet-screening methods;
Mixer exposure;
Cross-chain bridge controls;
Foreign distributor relationships;

Sanctions-freezing capabilities;

Law-enforcement response times;

High-risk jurisdiction exposure; and

Procedures governing self-hosted wallets.

XXIV. Public access database and transparency limitations

Federal law requires the Federal Reserve Board to maintain a public, searchable database concerning master-account and service requests and indicate whether each request is:
Pending;
Approved;
Rejected; or
Withdrawn.
Federal Reserve access-request database
The database improves transparency but may not reveal:
Full application materials;
Risk assessments;

Political communications;

Conditions imposed;

Reasons for approval or denial;

Indirect sponsor-bank access;

Technical-service relationships;

Affiliate structures;
Informal pre-application contacts; or
Applications not yet formally accepted as complete.
Those materials should be obtained separately.
XXV. Political financing and access decisions

A master account or equivalent direct-access arrangement could be worth enormous sums to a stablecoin issuer or exchange.

Investigators should compare application and decision dates against:
Campaign contributions;
Fairshake spending;
Lobbying contacts;
White House meetings;
Regulatory appointments;
Enforcement dismissals;

Charter approvals;

Acquisitions of eligible banks;

Ethics waivers;

Executive-order drafting; and

Federal Reserve personnel changes.

Political activity is lawful. A criminal concern arises only if a benefit was offered or accepted in exchange for a particular access decision or other official act.
XXVI. DOGE nexus
The DOGE inquiry should seek evidence showing whether DOGE personnel:
Participated in discussions about master-account eligibility;
Accessed Federal Reserve or Treasury payment-system information;
Identified regulatory barriers for particular crypto firms;
Communicated with applicants or their investors;
Recommended shortened review timelines;
Pressured regulators to modify adverse findings;

Shared nonpublic application information;

Helped structure bank acquisitions or charters;

Recommended government use of a privately issued token; or

Held undisclosed financial interests in an applicant, stablecoin, exchange, or venture investor.

Federal Reserve independence and DOGE’s exact legal authority over Reserve Banks must be carefully distinguished. The White House may request a review, but it cannot automatically rewrite statutory eligibility or order a Reserve Bank to maintain an unlawful account.
XXVII. Potential legal theories
If supported by evidence of corrupt or fraudulent conduct, possible violations could include:
Federal bribery;
Federal-program bribery;
Honest-services fraud involving bribery or kickbacks;

Wire or mail fraud;

Bank fraud;

False statements in an access application;
Concealment of beneficial ownership;
Procurement fraud;
Computer fraud;
Misappropriation of confidential supervisory information;
Money laundering;
Sanctions violations;
Obstruction;
Campaign-finance violations; or

Unlawful conflicts of interest.

RICO would require a qualifying enterprise, knowing participation, related predicate acts, continuity, and cognizable injury. Favorable regulation or account approval is not itself a predicate act.

XXVIII. Evidence required

Investigators should obtain:
The Federal Reserve’s Executive Order 14405 report;
All drafts and supporting analyses;
Board and Reserve Bank communications;

Master-account applications involving digital-asset firms;

Application status records;

Legal-eligibility memoranda;
Tier classifications;
Risk assessments;
Approval and denial letters;
Account restrictions and conditions;
Sponsor-bank agreements;
Correspondent-account records;
FedNow participation agreements;
Intraday-credit policies;
Collateral records;

Stablecoin reserve-account documentation;

Mint-to-settlement reconciliations;

Federal Reserve payment logs;

Charter applications;

Beneficial-ownership records;

White House communications;
Treasury communications;
DOGE and USDS communications;
Lobbying disclosures;
Political-contribution records;
Ethics waivers and recusals;
Enforcement and sanctions histories; and
Inspector-general or congressional reviews.

